Northwind Coffee Co. is a café and roaster in Halifax with three locations and a full menu with photos.

## Problem

At a café, the morning rush is the whole business. Customers who see a line walk on to the next place. Phone orders tie up the person on the till. And third-party ordering apps take a large cut of every order.

## How it helps your business

- **Shorter lines.** Regulars order on the way and walk straight to the pickup shelf.
- **No commission.** Orders come in on your own site, not through an app that takes a cut. Card orders pay Stripe's standard card fee and nothing more.
- **A calmer kitchen.** Pickup times are spread out and every order is on one screen.
- **Fewer "is my order ready?" questions,** because customers can see it on their phone.

## What it does

- **Order from a phone.** Customers pick a café, browse the menu with photos, and choose size, milk and extras. The price updates as they go.
- **Pay by card or at pickup.** Customers pay on Stripe's secure page (Apple Pay and Google Pay work too) or at the counter. A card order reaches the kitchen only once it's paid. If the café cancels it, the card is refunded.
- **Pickup times that respect the kitchen.** Customers only see times when the café is open and the kitchen has room. Each time slot takes a limited number of orders, so a rush doesn't pile up at 8:00.
- **A live kitchen screen.** A tablet shows New, Preparing and Ready columns. New orders arrive highlighted within seconds, with an optional chime. Staff move them along with one big tap, and late orders turn red.
- **"Ready for pickup" messages.** When staff tap Ready, the customer's order page turns green on its own. They also get an email, and a text if they asked for one.
- **QR menus.** Each café gets a printable menu QR code, and table cards that open the menu for that café and table.
- **The owner's view.** Today's orders by café, order value, a menu editor with options and prices, sold-out and seasonal items, and each café's own hours.
- **A support chat.** Customers can ask questions in a chat on the site.

## How it's built

- **Prices are checked on the server.** The basket lives in the browser, but every order is re-priced from the menu before it's saved, so nobody can change a price.
- **No overbooked pickup times.** Two phones racing for the last place in a time slot can't both get it; the second one sees fresh times.
- **Works on café Wi-Fi.** The kitchen screen checks for new orders every five seconds, which survives a flaky connection better than a live socket.
- **Payments without the risk.** Card details never touch the site: Stripe Checkout takes the payment, the amount comes from the server-priced order, and each payment is confirmed exactly once, however many times Stripe reports it. If a customer leaves the payment page, their basket is still there when they come back.

## Stack

Next.js (App Router), TypeScript, Tailwind CSS, Cloudflare Workers, Cloudflare D1 with Drizzle ORM, Stripe Checkout, Zod and Vitest. QR codes are drawn in the app, with no outside service.
